00:10:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes, you've boiled it down perfectly. Your checkout-line analogy is exactly right—and it's a much simpler way to say what I was getting at. The stomach doesn't have a clock or a timer. It doesn't think, "This bit of food has been here 75 minutes, I'll let it go." But because digestion takes time, the food that has been there longest is almost always the most physically broken down. So the stomach can use **"how digested is it?"** as a reliable stand-in for **"how old is it?"** Just like in a checkout line: the person at the front is usually the one who's been waiting longest, but the cashier doesn't need to know their arrival time—they just process whoever is next in line based on position. In the stomach, the position is determined by particle size and fluidity, which correlate closely with the time spent digesting. The only small nuance that makes the stomach a bit different from a strict checkout line is that not all foods break down at the same speed. A liquid (like water) can "skip the line" and be emptied almost immediately, even if you just drank it after a solid meal. And a fatty, solid meal might linger much longer than 2 hours. So the "time spent" isn't perfectly matched to "degree of breakdown," but in general, what you said is spot on: the stomach infers age by digestive state, and that's how it knows what's ready to go.
